  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nRemnants of Hurricane Isaias are now forecast to pass through \ncentral TN and eastern KY, with prospects of any significant \nprecipitation in southeast IL very low. Short-range ensembles \nnow encompassing the rainfall for central IL depict a maximum of\n30 percent chance of precipitation exceeding a quarter inch \nnear Lawrenceville, and 0 percent north of I-70. Timing looks to\nbe from early evening to around sunrise Sunday. \n\nBehind Isaias, a ridge will amplify into the Midwest, with dry \nconditions Sunday and Monday, followed by a Canadian shortwave \nsuppressing the ridge and driving a cold front through IL \nTuesday. This will bring a slight chance for showers and \nthunderstorms. A better chance for precipitation will develop \nmidweek and continue through Thursday as a deep central Rockies \nupper low produces a strong return of gulf moisture interacting \nwith the stalled cold front, followed by the Rockies low and a \nCanadian low merging and progressing toward the Midwest. The \neventual evolution of the system remains highly uncertain, with \nvarious models depicting much different scenarios by next \nweekend, but eventually suggesting a dry pattern by the weekend.\n\nTemperatures will be suppressed down a few degrees today and Sunday, \nout of near record territory, with upper 70s to mid 80s highs \nexpected. Strong warm advection Sunday into Monday should yield near \nrecord warmth on Monday, with upper 80s to lower 90s for highs. The \ncold front Tuesday should bring temperatures trending downward for \nmidweek, with temperatures down a few degrees for Tuesday, and highs \nin the 70s Wednesday and Thursday. Model blends trend toward further \ncooling Friday and Saturday, with highs in the 60s, but 5th to \n95th percentile 12Z ensemble range is from highs in the 40s to \n70s for Saturday.",
